The inverse half of the domain equation of Example 6.1 sends an element s of the sum of D with the product of two sharp copies to the sharp element whose neighbourhoods are Gamma, the zero embeddings of neighbourhoods that s meets through the left injection, and the pair embeddings of sharp neighbourhoods that s meets through the right injection of their product neighbourhood. Each of these is a sharp neighbourhood and Gamma is the master. Two such neighbourhoods intersect in one of the same three forms: Gamma absorbs either copy, zero copies intersect by the intersection law for left injections, pair copies intersect by the intersection law for right injections and product neighbourhoods, and a zero copy meets a pair copy in the empty set because the injections are disjoint and sum neighbourhoods are nonempty. An enlargement is Gamma or a copy of the same kind, because a zero embedding and a pair embedding cannot contain one another.
